    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The name Shiraz appears on Elamite tablets dated to 2000 BCE, but the city was founded or restored in 693 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> and stayed a provincial town until it became the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saffarid-dynasty">Saffarids</a> in the late 9th century.</li><li>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a>, who made it their capital, Shiraz became the largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a>, and in the 13th century it became a leading centre of the arts and letters, the home of Saadi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafez">Hafez</a>.</li><li>Spared by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Tamerlane</a>, Shiraz was sacked after a revolt against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a>, rose again as the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan Zand</a> from 1762, and was lowered to a provincial capital under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajars</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Shiraz was spared by both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Tamerlane</a>: its local ruler offered tribute and submission to the first, and in 1382 Shah Shoja agreed to submit to the second.</p></aside>
    </section>

