    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">2000 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Elamite clay tablets name a city called Tiraziš, the earliest reference to Shiraz</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">693</span><span class="rf-text">Shiraz is founded or restored by an Umayyad governor</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">894</span><span class="rf-text">Amr, a Saffarid ruler, founds the city's first congregational mosque</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">933</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a> oust the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saffarid-dynasty">Saffarids</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adud-al-dawla">Adud al-Dawla</a> later rules from Shiraz</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1504</span><span class="rf-text">Forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-i">Ismail I</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid dynasty</a>, capture the city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1762</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan Zand</a> makes Shiraz his capital</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1844</span><span class="rf-text">The Báb first declares his mission in Shiraz</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1910</span><span class="rf-text">A pogrom in the Jewish quarter kills 12 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Shiraz stands in the north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars province</a> in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, on a green plain at the foot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros Mountains</a>, 1,500 metres above sea level. A seasonal river called the Dry River flows through the northern part of the city. The earliest reference to the place is on Elamite clay tablets dated to 2000 BCE, found in June 1970 during digging for a brick kiln in the south-west corner of the city. They name a city called Tiraziš. Tablets from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persepolis">Persepolis</a> treasury archives, also in Elamite, mention major workshops in a place whose name is the ancient form of Shiraz, and the name appears on clay sealings found at the Sasanian ruin of Qasr-e Abu Nasr, east of the city.</p><p>There is no definitive record of the city before the late 7th century CE, but archaeological finds indicate that the site or its vicinity was settled as early as the 6th century BCE. Achaemenid and Sasanian remains lie around it, among them reliefs at Barm-e Delak and Guyim and Sasanian fortresses at Qasr-e Abu Nasr and Fahandezh. Scholars differ on what the ancient settlement was. Berney and Ring held that the lack of references in early Persian sources suggests no more than a way-station. Abdolmajed Arfaee pointed to its appearance in 84 tablets of the Persepolis Administrative Archives and to groups of workers as large as 490, and concluded that it was one of the most important settlements of the area.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/istakhr"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/istakhr.jpg" alt="Istakhr" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/istakhr">Istakhr</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Estakhr_Darafsh_(55).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">درفش کاویانی (Kaviani flag), CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Shiraz was founded or restored in 693, after Arab armies had conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a> in expeditions from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> between 640 and 653. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a> held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istakhr">Istakhr</a>, their capital in Fars, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> took it in 653. Because Istakhr was so closely tied to the Sasanian Empire and the Zoroastrian religion, the Arabs wanted a rival cultural and administrative centre nearby, and the new city was planned to be much larger than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>. The ambition was not realised, and until at least the late 9th century Shiraz remained what John Limbert called a provincial backwater. As Fars gradually turned from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and Istakhr declined, Shiraz became the practical centre of the province.</p><p>It grew most under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adud-al-dawla">Adud al-Dawla</a>, who ruled Fars between 949 and 983, had a large library, a hospital and several mosques, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bazaar">bazaars</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserais</a>, palaces and gardens built, and in 974 he built a fortified camp for his troops south of the city. One of his palaces stretched for nearly three miles and had 360 rooms. The city was divided into twelve quarters and had eight gates, and its prosperity came from the agricultural trade of Fars: grapes, linen, wool, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and rose and violet water. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-of-tudela">Benjamin of Tudela</a> reported 10,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> there in 1170. Its population was 60,000 in the 14th century and 200,000 in the 16th.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/imad-al-dawla"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/imad-al-dawla.jpg" alt="Imad al-Dawla" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/imad-al-dawla">Imad al-Dawla</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Imad_al-Dawla_coin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Classical Numismatic Group; [1], CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Arab Muslim army captured the area around Shiraz in 641, and the city was founded or restored in 693 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a>. As Abbasid authority weakened, regional dynasties arose. In the late 9th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saffarid-dynasty">Saffarids</a> under Ya'qub ibn al-Layth made Shiraz the capital of their autonomous state, which covered most of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imad-al-dawla">Imad al-Dawla</a> Ali ibn Buya ousted the Saffarids in 933. His nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adud-al-dawla">Adud al-Dawla</a> ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a> from 949 to 983 and in 977 added <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>, to his domains, so that the Abbasids became a puppet state of the Shiraz-based dynasty.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> did not destroy the city, because its local ruler offered tribute and submission to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Tamerlane</a> spared it in 1382 when Shah Shoja agreed to submit. In 1504 the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-i">Ismail I</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid dynasty</a>, captured Shiraz, which remained a provincial capital throughout the Safavid period. Emam Qoli Khan, governor of Fars under Abbas the Great, built palaces and ornate buildings in the style of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan Zand</a> made Shiraz his capital in 1762. Agha Mohammad Khan, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar dynasty</a>, destroyed its fortifications, and under the Qajars its governorship was a royal prerogative. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Pahlavi</a> era the tombs of Saadi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafez">Hafez</a> were constructed and presented to the public.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a> two Zoroastrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fire-temple">fire temples</a> still stood in Shiraz for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> who had not converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. The city had long had large Jewish and Christian communities. In the 20th century about 20,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> lived there, most of whom emigrated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and Israel in the latter half of the century, and the city also has a significant Baháʼí population. Most of the people are Persians and Shia Muslims. The crafts of Shiraz include inlaid mosaic work of triangular design, silverware, pile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-carpet">carpet-weaving</a> and the weaving of kilims, and under the Buyids it was a market where rug weavers and painters sold their products.</p><p>In the 13th century Shiraz became a leading centre of the arts and letters, thanks to the encouragement of its ruler and the presence of Persian scholars and artists, and classical geographers named it Dar al-'Elm, the House of Knowledge. Saadi, the poet of the 12th and 13th centuries, was born there. After studying at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizamiyya">Nizamiyya</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> he returned as an old man to a city that was, under the Atabak Abubakr Sa'd ibn Zangy (1231 to 1260), enjoying relative tranquillity, and he seems to have spent the rest of his life there. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafez">Hafez</a>, the mystic Ruzbehan and the philosopher Mulla Sadra were also born in Shiraz, as was the 13th-century astronomer Qutb al-Din al-Shirazi. The Khan Theological School has tile-covered buildings dating from 1627. The city has a considerable number of gardens.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nader-shah.jpg" alt="Nader Shah" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Contemporary_portrait_of_Nader_Shah._Artist_unknown,_created_in_ca._1740_in_Iran_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a>, Shiraz declined. The raids of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a> made matters worse, and so did the rebellion of its governor against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a>, who sent troops to suppress it. The city was besieged for many months and eventually sacked. When Nader Shah was murdered in 1747, most of its historical buildings had been damaged or ruined, and its population had fallen to 55,000 in the mid-18th century by one estimate and to 50,000 by another, the lower figure being a quarter of the population of the 16th century.</p><p>The city recovered under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan Zand</a>, who employed more than 12,000 workers to build a royal district with a fortress, administrative buildings, a mosque and a covered bazaar, and who also built a moat and an irrigation and drainage system and rebuilt the walls. His heirs failed to hold what he had gained. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agha-mohammad-khan-qajar">Agha Mohammad Khan Qajar</a> took his revenge on the city by destroying its fortifications, destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lak">Lak</a> quarter that had been established in the Zand period and moved the national capital away from Shiraz.</p><p>In 1910 a pogrom began in the Jewish quarter after false rumours that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> had ritually murdered a Muslim girl. In the riots 12 Jews were killed and about 50 injured, and the 6,000 Jews of Shiraz were robbed of all their possessions. In the 1930s the opening of the trans-Iranian railway shifted trade routes to the ports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuzestan">Khuzestan</a>, and much of the city's architectural inheritance, especially the Zand royal district, was neglected or destroyed under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Pahlavis</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/vakil-mosque"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vakil-mosque.jpg" alt="Vakil Mosque" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/vakil-mosque">Vakil Mosque</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Masdjed-e_Vakil_central_court_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dynamosquito from France, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Shiraz is remembered as the city of the poets Saadi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafez">Hafez</a>, whose tombs lie on the north side of the present city, and has been nicknamed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. Other tombs include those of Khwaju Kermani, Shah Shoja and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan Zand</a>. The Atigh Jame' Mosque, founded by the Saffarid Amr in 894, is the city's oldest mosque, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vakil-mosque">Vakil Mosque</a> and the Nasir al-Mulk mosque followed it. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arg-of-karim-khan">Arg of Karim Khan</a>, a citadel, stands beside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vakil-bazaar">Vakil Bazaar</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vakil-bath">Vakil Bath</a> in the centre. The Qur'an Gate marks the entrance to the city.</p><p>Shiraz is also a holy city for Baháʼís. The Báb, Siyyid 'Ali-Muhammad, was born there in 1819, and on the evening of 22 May 1844 he first declared his mission there as the bearer of a new divine revelation, according to the Baháʼí account. His house became a place of pilgrimage and was destroyed in 1979. Another account says that on that evening he began discussions that led to his claiming to be an interpreter of the Qur'an.</p><p>Shiraz is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars province</a> and the fifth most populous city of Iran. Wine, gardens and nightingales have long been associated with it, and the city's Shirazi wine is said to originate there.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Shiraz, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars province</a> in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, appears on Elamite tablets of 2000 BCE and was founded or restored in 693. It was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saffarid-dynasty">Saffarids</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zand-dynasty">Zands</a>, and in the 13th century became a leading centre of the arts and letters, the home of Saadi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafez">Hafez</a>.</em></p>

